 Creating a complex smart contract involves writing code that follows the rules of a specific blockchain platform, such as Ethereum, Solana, or EOS. Here's an example of a simple smart contract written in Solidity for the Ethereum blockchain. This contract demonstrates a basic crowdfunding mechanism, where users can send Ether to the contract and receive tokens in return.

```solidity
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract Crowdfunding {
    address payable public owner;
    uint256 public tokensPerEther;
    mapping(address => uint256) public balanceOf;

    constructor() {
        owner = msg.sender;
        tokensPerEther = 1000;
    }

    function sendCrowdfunding() public payable {
        require(msg.value > 0, "You must send some Ether.");
        require(msg.sender != owner, "The owner cannot participate.");

        uint256 newTokens = msg.value * tokensPerEther;
        balanceOf[msg.sender] += newTokens;
        (bool success, ) = msg.sender.transfer(address(this).balance - msg.value);
        require(success, "Transfer of Ether failed.");
    }

    function withdraw() public {
        require(msg.sender == owner, "Only the owner can withdraw.");
        msg.sender.transfer(address(this).balance);
    }

    function totalTokens() public view returns (uint256) {
        uint256 total = 0;
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < balanceOf.length; i++) {
            total += balanceOf[i];
        }
        return total;
    }

    function balanceOf(address _owner) public view returns (uint256) {
        return balanceOf[_owner];
    }
}
```

This contract allows users to send Ether to the contract address, and in return, they receive tokens proportional to the amount of Ether they sent. The contract also includes a function for the owner to withdraw the Ether sent to the contract. The contract also provides functions to view the total number of tokens and the balance of a specific address.

This is a simple example, and a real-world complex smart contract would likely include additional features, such as token distribution, token burning, and more complex access control mechanisms. It's essential to thoroughly test and audit any smart contract before deploying it to a live blockchain.

